# Recall — write what the repository cannot say
For task requests and corrections, follow the shared [request continuity protocol](../../runtime/TASKS.md) once per session.
## The test for writing something down
**Would the repository already tell me this?** If yes, do not write it. Code
structure, past fixes, commit history, and the conventions file are already
durable, and a copy of them is a second source that goes stale on its own
schedule.
| Worth persisting | Already in the repo |
| --- | --- |
| Why a rejected approach was rejected, when the code shows no trace | What the code does |
| A constraint that came from outside the codebase — a person, a contract, an outage | The commit that fixed a bug |
| A preference the user stated once and expects to hold | Which files exist and how they are laid out |
| A resource that lives elsewhere — a dashboard, a ticket, an account | Anything the conventions file already states |
The first row is the one worth the effort. Reasons evaporate; outcomes do not.
## One fact per record, with its reason
A record holding three facts cannot be corrected — updating one means rewriting
the others, so it drifts as a whole or not at all. Write one, and link to the
neighbours rather than restating them.
Write the **reason** into the record, not only the conclusion. "Use the Python
path, not Node" is unfollowable when the reader hits a case it does not cover;
"…because the Node build has no CJK grapheme handling" is.
Convert relative dates. "Last week" is wrong on every later read; the date is
right forever.
## A memory is evidence about the past, not the present
What was written was true when it was written. If a record names a file, a flag,
or a command, **check it still exists before recommending it** — the check costs
a moment and its absence costs a confident wrong answer.
This is the whole risk of recall: a stale record is more dangerous than no
record, because it arrives with the authority of something already decided.
Signals that a record needs re-checking before use are in
`references/staleness.md`, together with what to do when it turns out wrong.
## Prefer correcting over appending
When something recorded turns out wrong, fix or delete that record. Appending a
correction next to it leaves two records that disagree, and the next reader has
no way to tell which one won — a contradiction is worse than either statement
alone.
Deleting a record that is no longer true is not losing information. Keeping a
false one is.
## Do not record progress
Task state belongs where the task lives, not in durable memory. A record saying
"currently refactoring the parser" is false within a day and stays retrievable
for months, which is the exact shape that makes memory untrustworthy.
## Recovering interrupted work from live state
Conversation memory may help locate an interrupted task, but it cannot settle
the task's current state. First resolve the current working directory,
repository root, and exact ref. Then read the live status, diff, task artifact,
and the newest relevant test or build evidence before writing a recovery brief.
Never recover from conversation memory alone.
If `.jstack/tasks/<task-id>/task.json` exists, resume through the repository
record instead of rebuilding state from a prior completion message:
```bash
node "${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/verify/scripts/workflow.js" resume <task-id>
node "${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/verify/scripts/workflow.js" status <task-id>
```
`resume` checks repository identity, live HEAD and Git status, then re-hashes
the recorded cursor and evidence. Changed or missing recovery evidence stops the
resume. A successful resume still sets fresh verification required; it does not
restore a previous `complete` word. `status` rechecks the immutable contract,
latest receipt, requirement files, and review evidence against live bytes.
| Conversation says | Live evidence | Recovery result |
|---|---|---|
| Work is complete | Dirty diff or failing check | Incomplete; report the live state and the conflict |
| Work is incomplete | Clean candidate and fresh passing evidence | Re-verify, then it may be marked complete |
| Any claim | No supporting file, commit, or artifact | Unknown; do not repeat the claim |
The live state wins when it conflicts with the conversation. Every recovered
claim must point to a file, commit, artifact, or fresh command result. If the
evidence is missing, label the claim unknown and leave it out of the handoff.
## Explicit native session migration
For a requested project-path or profile migration, read
`references/session-migration.md` and use `scripts/migrate_sessions.js`.
Choose the data format with `--host claude` or `--host codex`, independently of
which harness is executing this skill. Migration is an explicit mode: ordinary
memory searches and interrupted-task recovery must not modify native session storage.
Use explicit source and destination profiles, plan before applying, and retain the
receipt for verification or rollback. Do not continue into memory export setup.
## Finding what a past session said
Memory records are one source; the sessions themselves are another, and they are
neither the repository nor a record somebody chose to write. They exist only if
the export pipeline is installed, and they are answered from **two stores**:
| What is being asked | Store | How |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The last N sessions, today, yesterday | SQLite index | `sessions` table ordered by `session_datetime` |
| Everything touching one project | SQLite index | same table filtered by `project` |
| A keyword, an error string, a decision | Markdown corpus | content search over the exported sessions |
| One session end to end | Markdown corpus | read the export named by its session id |
The rule is one line: **time and metadata go to the index, content goes to the
corpus.** Asking the index for content finds nothing — it stores a 100-character
summary, not the transcript. Asking the corpus for "the last five" ranks by
relevance and quietly answers a different question.
Both stores sit under one variable, and it is the only path this skill knows:
```bash
SESSIONS="${CLAUDE_SESSIONS_PATH:-$HOME/Documents/Obsidian/claude-code-sessions}"
sqlite3 "$SESSIONS/.metadata.db" \
"SELECT session_datetime, project, session_id, summary FROM sessions
WHERE session_datetime <> '' ORDER BY session_datetime DESC LIMIT 5;"
```
`$SESSIONS/sessions/<session-id>.md` is the export; `$SESSIONS/.metadata.db` is
the index. Never hardcode either — a second profile moves both.
## The pipeline that fills those stores
`bin/` holds the producer: a Rust binary that walks the session logs, converts
each to markdown, and upserts the index. It is not a search tool — it runs at
`SessionEnd`, and nothing it has not seen is findable.
Three things must be true before any query above returns rows, and they are
installed by the plugin's `/setup-recall`, not by this skill: the binary is
**built**, it is wired to the `SessionEnd` hook, and content search is pointed
at `$SESSIONS/sessions`. The producer resolves the session log directory from
the active profile: a nonempty `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` gets `/projects` appended,
otherwise it uses `~/.claude/projects`. The output vault remains controlled by
`CLAUDE_SESSIONS_PATH`. The binary is built from source, so a machine without a
toolchain has an index that never advances.
**An empty result is not evidence that the work never happened.** Check that the
index exists and when it last advanced before reporting nothing found — the
answer "the pipeline is not installed here" and the answer "you never did that"
look identical from the query side, and only one of them is usually true. This
is the same failure as a stale record: absence arriving with the authority of a
settled fact.
## Boundary
Recording an architecture decision so the codebase carries it is the `decide`
skill — that is a document in the repository, not a memory. This skill covers
what persists outside it.
## Profile maintenance boundary
The `agent-maintenance` skill handles profile selection, plugin ghost cleanup and
project-migration target selection. Recall retains its explicit migration entry and
shared engine for compatibility. For combined maintenance requests use that skill's
branch contract; ordinary historical searches here do not mutate native storage.
